WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:04.200
Horror films are supposed to scare us, it's
right there in the name of the genre, after

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.200
all.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:08.960
The whole reason we line up to watch horror
movies is to experience the safe thrill of

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:11.900
being terrified by fictional objects on a
screen.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.460
It's all totally fake, of course, but there
have been times when the scares hit a little

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:19.310
too close to home, and some of the scariest
movie scenes have even managed to frighten

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:21.690
the actors and actresses bringing them to
life.

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:26.580
Here are some iconic movie scenes that scared
the pants off the actors in real life.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:27.829
Alien

00:00:27.829 --> 00:00:32.310
When that tiny alien burst out of John Hurt's
chest in Alien, it scared everyone, including

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:33.310
the cast.

00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:36.950
Although the special effects themselves were
pretty shocking, the real magic of this scene

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:39.370
is how surprised and terrified the cast appears.

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:41.610
These horrified faces are genuine.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:45.390
John Hurt was taken aside during shooting
and rigged up to a device loaded with rancid

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:46.390
pig organs.

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:50.100
The rest of the cast was brought in unaware
of the device about to spray them with the

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:54.410
organs, or why the film crew was wearing rain
gear and protective plastic sheeting.

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:58.500
The first take strategically brought the crew
closer to the table by spurting only a small

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:02.750
amount of fake blood, so when the next take
left them all covered in blood, their faces

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:04.870
expressed true, terrified surprise.

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:12.070
"And there's a master where all five of us
are like… and we're not acting."

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:13.500
The Texas Chainsaw Massacre

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:19.570
The 1974 horror classic The Texas Chainsaw
Massacre is another example of a director

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:24.730
strategically designing scenarios to elicit
real terror from the actors and actresses.

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:28.550
Director Tobe Hooper kept his star Leatherface,
played by Gunnar Hansen, out of sight from

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:32.390
the rest of the crew, so his mask would be
a most horrifying surprise.

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:36.010
Specifically, when Jerry runs into him for
the first time in the kitchen, his high pitched

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:38.530
squeal is absolutely real.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:40.880
The Blair Witch Project

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:44.780
This entire film seems to have been planned
as an elaborate scheme to slowly push the

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:46.330
cast into going insane.

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:50.790
The directors of The Blair Witch Project gave
the three cast members cameras, supplies for

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:52.560
survival, and a place to meet up.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.130
From there, the three were essentially left
alone in the woods.

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:59.000
A few scenes from the movie are from when
they were actually lost trying to figure out

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:00.030
next meet up point.

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:04.550
Often times the directors would secretly follow
the cast and break sticks or throw rocks around

00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:05.550
the unknowing campers.

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:10.190
"And every once in awhile when it was dark,
you'd see this red beam and you knew it was

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:12.810
the hacksome band of goons running around
again."

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:16.980
Worse yet, the directors gave them less food
everyday to make them ragged, angry, and more

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:17.980
on edge.

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:21.890
And as the days went on, the filmmakers came
up with new ways to torture their actors.

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:26.460
"And they were setting up sound loops of babies
crying and kids laughing outside of your tent

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:27.460
at 4AM in the morning."

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:31.420
"Basically it was just us running through
trees going nuts out there."

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:36.390
After eight days of filming, it's safe to
say that the cast no longer enjoys walks in

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:37.390
the woods.

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:38.390
Prometheus

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:41.370
After Alien, you'd think actors would all
be on red alert anytime Ridley Scott makes

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:42.370
a monster flick.

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:46.640
But the actors on Prometheus evidently thought
the director's trickster days were over, allowing

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:48.550
Scott to get in one more scare.

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:53.069
In one of Prometheus's more infamous scenes,
a biologist tries petting the world's scariest

00:02:53.069 --> 00:02:54.069
space cobra.

00:02:54.069 --> 00:02:55.540
It doesn't end well.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:57.880
"Cut it off, cut it off!"

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:01.710
A little later, his friends stumble across
his corpse, and when they turn his body over,

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:03.680
the monster shoots up out of his mouth.

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:05.300
"Ahh!"

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:09.460
As you've probably guessed by this point,
those screams are genuine.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:14.300
Pulling pretty much the exact same stunt he
used more than 30 years ago, Scott replaced

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:18.700
actor Rafe Spall with a dummy, loaded the
alien puppet in its mouth, and connected the

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:20.530
alien creature to a wire.

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:24.629
He then told everyone on set to keep quiet
and make sure the actors didn't see the storyboards

00:03:24.629 --> 00:03:25.629
for the day.

00:03:25.629 --> 00:03:29.030
When actress Kate Dickie rolled the corpse
over, a crew member yanked the monster out

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:31.770
of the dummy's mouth, causing Dickie to yelp
in alarm.

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:36.230
Even tough guy Idris Elba, who was standing
nearby, let out a squeal of surprise.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:37.230
The Monster Squad

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:39.140
Directed by Fred Dekker and co-written by
Shane Black, The Monster Squad is a unique

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:43.239
'80s movie that pits a group of kids against
some of the most famous monsters in cinematic

00:03:43.239 --> 00:03:44.239
history.

00:03:44.239 --> 00:03:47.480
Of course, the scariest of the bunch is Count
Dracula himself.

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:51.440
Played by Duncan Regehr, this Transylvanian
vampire wants to get his undead hands on an

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:53.560
amulet that will help him conquer the world.

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:57.920
However, standing between the count and unlimited
power is one 5-year-old girl.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:01.739
As the heroes battle it out with the monsters,
Dracula approaches the girl, lifts her up

00:04:01.739 --> 00:04:05.630
by her chin, and angrily demands she hand
over the all-powerful jewel.

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:09.810
The count then reveals his red eyes and pearly
whites, causing the 5-year-old to scream in

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:10.660
terror.

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:12.480
*HISS*!!

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:17.100
While it works perfectly for the scene, the
young actress really was afraid.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:20.900
It was the first time she had seen Regehr
in his full vampire regalia, and the sight

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:24.210
of those fangs was more than enough to make
her freak out.

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:25.590
The Godfather

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:29.560
Believe it or not, some of the very best scenes
in The Godfather happened in the spur of the

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:30.560
moment.

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:32.280
For example, remember Vito Corleone's cat?

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:36.240
Director Francis Ford Coppola just found that
feline wandering around the studio and decided

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:37.490
to put it in the scene.

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:41.180
And when Vito slaps Johnny Fontane across
the face, that was Brando trying to shake

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:44.110
up actor Al Martino and put some life into
the scene.

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:48.220
The legendary method actor was also doing
a little improv in his final scene.

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:52.370
Moments before Vito Corleone falls down dead,
he's playing with his grandson, Anthony.

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:55.590
Obviously, toddlers aren't exactly natural
actors.

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:59.320
Since Brando needed to connect with the kid,
he put an orange peel in his mouth and began

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:00.750
growling like a monster.

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:03.540
But this didn't sit well with the young actor
playing Anthony.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:08.360
"Ahhh!"

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:14.000
The kid was actually scared, forcing Brando
to drop the game and comfort the kid.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:18.210
It's a nice touch to a sad scene, a unique
moment of improvisation that makes The Godfather

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:20.280
one of the all-time greats.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:22.310
Close Encounters of the Third Kind

00:05:22.310 --> 00:05:26.031
While Richard Dreyfuss is the main character
of Close Encounters of the Third Kind, it's

00:05:26.031 --> 00:05:28.950
Cary Guffey who steals the scene every time
he shows up.

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:32.610
The adorable 4-year-old played the part of
Barry Guiler, the little boy who's abducted

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:33.699
by aliens.

00:05:33.699 --> 00:05:37.340
All his reactions seem so genuine … and
that's because they are.

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:41.240
Throughout shooting, director Steven Spielberg
was constantly coming up with ways to encourage

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:42.240
Guffey's performance.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:46.791
For example, in the scene where the boy is
smiling out the window at the UFOs, Spielberg

00:05:46.791 --> 00:05:49.360
is off-screen, unwrapping presents for Guffey.

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:53.840
Later on, when Barry tearfully tells his alien
friends goodbye, he's really crying because

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:57.020
Spielberg told him to imagine his friends
leaving forever.

00:05:57.020 --> 00:06:01.270
But perhaps Spielberg's most ingenious ploy
comes earlier in the film, when Barry runs

00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:05.090
into his kitchen and spots the alien rummaging
through his refrigerator.

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:09.430
The space invaders are kept off-camera, and
the whole scene plays off Barry's face.

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:12.990
In a matter of seconds, we see the boy go
from fright to joy.

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:14.940
So what's he actually looking at?

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:19.199
Spielberg had makeup man Bob Westmoreland
dress up as a gorilla and hide behind a cardboard

00:06:19.199 --> 00:06:20.199
partition.

00:06:20.199 --> 00:06:23.570
When Guffey ran into the room, the partition
was pulled away and he was face-to-face with

00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:24.570
a scary ape.

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:27.510
But then the director told Westmoreland to
take off his mask.

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:31.780
Suddenly, Guffey recognized the makeup man
and broke out into a big smile.

00:06:31.780 --> 00:06:35.210
Spielberg explained: "Even though it was a
trick, the reaction was pure and honest."

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:39.240
Really, that's a phrase you could use to describe
almost every Spielberg film.

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:41.539
Willy Wonka and the Chocolate Factory

00:06:41.539 --> 00:06:44.900
There's no denying that Willy Wonka and the
Chocolate Factory is a childhood classic,

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:47.580
but honestly, it's one of the freakiest films
ever made.

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:49.270
And that's all thanks to Gene Wilder.

00:06:49.270 --> 00:06:53.180
Sure, his version of Wonka is hilarious and
even occasionally soft-hearted, but there's

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:55.150
just something off about the guy.

00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:58.070
You never know when he's going to truly lose
his mind.

00:06:58.070 --> 00:06:59.210
"You lose!

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:00.920
Good day sir!"

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:04.960
Of course, audiences weren't the only ones
who found Gene Wilder's maniacal candy man

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:05.960
frightening.

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:09.440
As it turns out, the frizzy-haired actor also
terrified his young co-stars.

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:13.360
While working on the film, director Mel Stuart
liked to keep all the kids guessing, hiding

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:16.840
key details from them until it was actually
time to shoot a scene.

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:20.780
For example, Stuart kept the actors away from
the famous Chocolate Room until he was ready

00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:21.889
to roll the camera.

00:07:21.889 --> 00:07:26.630
So when Charlie and company step into Wonka's
wonderland, their looks of awe are genuine.

00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:31.010
But then there's the infamous boat scene,
widely regarded as the scariest part of the

00:07:31.010 --> 00:07:32.010
film.

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:35.030
Stuart didn't tell anyone what exactly was
going to happen during that boat ride.

00:07:35.030 --> 00:07:39.340
So when Wilder started going on about hurricanes
blowing and the grisly reaper mowing, the

00:07:39.340 --> 00:07:40.920
kids were scared out of their minds.

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:48.820
"And they're certainly not showing any signs
that they are slowing!"

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:53.780
Actress Denise Nickerson, who played Violet
Beauregarde, actually thought Wilder had gone

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:54.780
mad.

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:57.949
Really, that's a testament to Wilder's acting
abilities, and while he's appeared in several

00:07:57.949 --> 00:08:02.020
other iconic films, it's safe to say he'll
always be best remembered for this candy-coated

00:08:02.020 --> 00:08:02.750
creepfest.

